Encapsulation in Python is an Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) concept where data (attributes) and methods (functions) are bundled together inside a class, and access to the data is controlled to protect it from unintended interference or misuse.

It helps in:

	Data hiding (restricting direct access to variables)
	Better maintainability
	Controlled access via getter/setter methods

Key Points
	Public Members – Accessible from anywhere.
	Protected Members – Prefix with a single underscore _var (convention: should not be accessed directly outside the class).
	Private Members – Prefix with double underscore __var (name mangling makes them harder to access directly).

SYNTAX ::::

class student:
    def __init__(self):
        self.__name=''
        self.__mobile=0
        self.__classname=''
        self.__subject=''

    def get_name(self):
        return self.name
    def set_name(self,name):
        self.name=name

    def get_mobile(self):
        return self.mobile
    def set_mobile(self,mobile):
        self.mobile=mobile

    def get_classname(self):
        return self.classname
    def set_classname(self,classname):
        self.classname=classname

    def get_subject(self):
        return self.subject
    def set_subject(self,subject):
        self.subject=subject

obj=student()
obj.set_name("KUSH")
obj.set_mobile(9876543211)
obj.set_classname("BCA")
obj.set_subject("Maths")

print(obj.get_name())
print(obj.get_mobile())
print(obj.get_classname())
print(obj.get_subject())


